     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg of ibuprofen a day.\n- The usual maximum single dose is 800 mg. This order is twice that.\n- The usual maximum daily dose for adults, even under supervision, is 3,200 mg. This order is twice that.\n- At this dose there's a high risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and heart problems, even in a healthy 70 kg adult.\n\nThis may be a typo or a mix-up. For example, someone may have meant 400 mg four times daily (1,600 mg/day) or 800 mg three times daily.\n\n**2. Medication orders need to come from a prescriber.** This tool is for clinicians entering orders for their patients. You've said you're not a clinician, so I shouldn't enter a medication order for you, even with a corrected dose. That safeguard protects the patient and the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team's prescriber. They can confirm what was intended and enter or authorize the order.\n- If you're passing this along from a clinician, ask them to check the dose. The ward pharmacist can also help clarify it.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms a safe dose, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
